program            = 005_struct_copy_and_return
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = By-value aggregate copy, parameter passing and return on both sides of the by-register and by-memory threshold, folded and runtime variants, every member read back individually
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every member of every aggregate is assigned before it is read. make_small assigns both
members of its local before returning it, and make_large assigns all eight members of its
local before returning it, so no returned aggregate carries an indeterminate member. doubled
receives its parameter by value, and a by-value parameter is a fully initialized copy of the
argument, so reading and then modifying v is well defined. consume_first and consume_last
likewise read only members of a fully initialized by-value copy. The plain assignment copy of
sf into copy copies an object whose members were both written by make_small. All arithmetic is
on small int values far from the int range edges, so there is no signed overflow: the largest
intermediate value is 26. No shift is performed and no pointer arithmetic is performed. No
object is modified twice between sequence points, and no printf call receives a side-effecting
argument, so unspecified evaluation order cannot affect the output. There is no aliasing
violation: every object is accessed only through an lvalue of its own declared type, and the
two struct types are never mixed or reinterpreted. No padding byte is read or compared,
because neither aggregate contains any padding at all -- see the implementation-defined notes.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
NO PADDING BY CONSTRUCTION. Every member of struct small2 and struct large8 is an int, so both
aggregates are exact multiples of sizeof(int) with no internal or trailing padding on any of
the four targets. This is deliberate rather than incidental: padding byte contents are
unspecified by the standard, so a program that printed or compared them would generate
divergences that say nothing about compiler correctness. No member is char, short or double
for exactly this reason.

WIDTH NORMALIZATION. Both printed sizes are of all-int aggregates: sizeof(struct small2) is
two ints (8 bytes) and sizeof(struct large8) is eight ints (32 bytes). Both are identical on
x86_64, i686, aarch64 and riscv64, because int is 4 bytes on all four. No sizeof of long,
pointer, double, long long or any aggregate containing them is printed, so the measured i686
difference (4-byte long and pointer versus 8 bytes elsewhere) cannot surface. No address or
pointer value is printed. Plain char is not used, so the measured char signedness difference
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is irrelevant. The only printf
conversion specifier used in the program is %d.

BOTH SIDES OF THE ABI THRESHOLD. struct small2 at 8 bytes and struct large8 at 32 bytes sit on
opposite sides of the by-register versus by-memory threshold on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64,
where that threshold is 16 bytes, so on those three backends both aggregate-passing code paths
are exercised. On i686 the two sit on the same side of it: that ABI passes every aggregate on
the stack and returns one through a hidden pointer whatever its size, which was measured here
rather than assumed, so both sizes travel i686's single memory path. Nothing is excluded on any
target either way, because what the oracles compare is the member values that come back out and
not the route they took. Every member is read back individually rather than summarized, so a
single wrong member localizes to a single wrong field. The passthrough line is the most
diagnostic in the program: it asserts both that the callee's mutation appeared in its return
value and that the caller's original object was left unchanged, which a backend passing the
aggregate by reference instead of by value would violate visibly.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
small_folded=1 2
small_runtime=4 5
large_folded=1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
large_runtime=4 5 6 7 8 9 10 11
copy=1 2
passthrough=2 4 unchanged=1 2
consume=10 26
sizes=8 32
END
